>h2 Overview
>tree open Overview
The CupidC and CupidLD ELF path is separate from the CupidC source JIT.
Linux runs the checked i386 Linux seed directly. Windows runs it through WSL.
An optional native Windows check requires CupidC and CupidLD output to match
the seed.

kernel/lang/exec.cc currently supports:
- ELF32 executables
- i386 machine type
- ET_EXEC
- PT_LOAD segment loading

ELF programs are started as external processes and receive the syscall table
pointer as the first argument to _start.
>endtree

>h2 Checked-Seed Windows Image Proof
>tree open Checked-Seed Windows Image Proof
Checked-seed CupidLD also serializes one fixed-layout i386 PE32 console image.
It can build canonical import descriptors, lookup tables, IAT cells, and names
in writable .idata. Imported slots accept only known, zero-addend absolute
relocations. CupidLD orders imports with an in-place heap and keeps name
imports below the PE32 high-bit boundary. The fixed image has no
base-relocation table. The independent validator reconstructs the exact
.idata cursor and rejects equivalent but noncanonical layouts.

CupidASM assembles the repository Windows entry, and freestanding CupidC
compiles its headerless main. CupidLD links GetStdHandle, WriteFile, and
ExitProcess imports. Windows validates and runs the image, requires the exact
stdout marker, empty stderr, and exit 37. The bootstrap report retains the
observed result and both stages' object and image hashes. The promoted seed
carries this path. Its tools are still static i386 Linux programs that run
through WSL on Windows, so this is not a native Windows fixed point.
>endtree

>h2 Entry Point
>tree open Entry Point
Use the wrapper header:
  user/cupid.h

Canonical entry:
  void _start(cupid_syscall_table_t *sys) {
      cupid_init(sys);
      ...
      exit();
  }

cupid_init(sys) stores the syscall table so wrapper functions can forward to
the kernel.
>endtree

>h2 Loader Constraints
>tree open Loader Constraints
kernel/lang/exec.cc applies these loader checks:
- ELF32 only
- little-endian
- executable type required
- i386 only
- at least one PT_LOAD segment (max ELF_MAX_PHDRS = 16)
- ordinary external ELF images must fit the exclusive 0x01C00000-0x01E00000
  arena, so their maximum span is 2 MB
- CupidC AOT images remain accepted in 0x01100000-0x01A00000
- CupidASM AOT images remain accepted in 0x01A00000-0x01C00000
- the complete PT_LOAD range must stay inside one accepted arena
- the entry point must be inside file-backed bytes of a PF_X PT_LOAD segment

If the binary violates those rules, exec rejects it.
>endtree

>h2 Build Flow
>tree open Build Flow
The user-space examples live under:
- user/examples/hello.cc
- user/examples/ls.cc
- user/examples/cat.cc

Build them with:
  make -C user

The current user/Makefile writes outputs to:
  user/build/

The build freezes its CupidC and CupidLD tool images, validates each object
and executable, and publishes only complete files. Linux runs the checked
seed directly, while Windows runs it through WSL. Use
`make test-user-cupidc-frontier` to repeat the deterministic build check. On
Windows, `make test-user-native-windows-equivalence` builds private native
driver snapshots and compares all six files with checked-seed output.

Run `make sync-user` to build the image and stage the examples at FAT root.
A fresh image imports them into /home on first boot. Once HOMEFS.SYS exists,
later host-staged FAT files appear under /disk and must be run there or copied
into /home from the guest.
>endtree

>h2 Running ELF Programs
>tree open Running ELF Programs
Run a compiled binary by path:
  exec /home/hello

You can also disassemble instead of run:
  exec -d /home/hello

The shell prints the started PID when loading succeeds.
>button ls /home | shell:ls /home
>button ls /home/bin | shell:ls /home/bin
>endtree

>h2 Syscall Wrapper Surface
>tree open Syscall Wrapper Surface
user/cupid.h exposes wrappers around the syscall table for:
- console I/O
- memory allocation
- string helpers
- VFS file and directory calls
- process helpers
- shell execution
- uptime/time queries
- program execution

These wrappers expose the syscall table directly. CupidC instead uses
pre-registered bindings.

The current external-program table ABI is version 5. It contains 103
four-byte fields and occupies 412 bytes on i386. The version and table size
come first, followed by 101 kernel function pointers.

The public i386 scalar widths are:
- uint8_t: 1 byte, unsigned
- uint16_t: 2 bytes, unsigned
- uint32_t: 4 bytes, unsigned
- int32_t: 4 bytes, signed
- size_t: 4 bytes, unsigned

VFS names may use 128 bytes and paths may use 512 bytes. cupid_dirent_t is
136 bytes, with name at byte 0, size at byte 128, and type at byte 132.
cupid_stat_t is 8 bytes, with size at byte 0 and type at byte 4.

Before compiling a tracked example, the build captures the exact bytes of
the six kernel and public declarations that define this ABI. It compares the
reviewed i386 layout and rechecks every input before reporting success.
>endtree

>h2 Process Model
>tree open Process Model
ELF programs are created through process_create_with_arg_image_ex(...) with:
  PROCESS_DOMAIN_EXTERNAL

Image ownership is transferred into the process before its PID is published.
Ordinary external programs hold one generation-tagged exclusive arena lease;
cleanup releases the lease but never frees the permanently reserved pages.

The process table classifies ELF programs separately, but all programs still
run in one shared ring-0 address space.
>button ps | shell:ps
>endtree

>h2 Checked Build Reference
>tree open Checked Build Reference
The production path accepts the three tracked example sources and their exact
header closure. It uses the USER_I386 profile, then links with entry symbol
_start at the fixed external base. Source, header, tool-image, or control-file
drift stops publication.

Build and check all three examples:
  make test-user-cupidc-frontier

Compare native Windows output with the checked seed:
  make test-user-native-windows-equivalence

The local user/build directory is generated and ignored by Git. Rebuild the
programs before staging an image.

Stage them into the active image:
  make sync-user

Run the complete guest check:
  make test-user-cupidc-runtime

The 0x01C00000 base follows CupidASM's fixed arena. The kernel stack and the
CupidC and CupidASM ranges remain below it. The loader leases the external
arena exclusively because fixed-address programs cannot overlap.

Programs linked at an earlier fixed base must be rebuilt.

The runtime gate boots and executes hello, ls, and cat separately. It checks
hello's greeting and numeric output and confirms that ls reads the shell's
root directory. Every run starts from a private copy of the same staged
image. The cat copy also receives the fixed /disk/catfix.txt fixture at
/home/readme.txt. Cat keeps its normal HomeFS path, the selected image stays
unchanged, and the fixture supplies text shaped like a serial marker.
External print calls record only their PID, byte count, and FNV-1a
fingerprint, so file contents cannot forge a passing event. Every run must
finish with an exit marker and process exit for the PID that the loader
assigned.

A separate lease gate starts the same external program twice at 0x01C00000.
The first process exits and releases the arena before the second process
claims it and loads at the same address.
>endtree

>h2 When To Use ELF Instead Of CupidC
>tree open When To Use ELF Instead Of CupidC
Choose ELF when you want:
- a checked, reproducible external build
- direct syscall-table style programming
- fixed binary artifacts on disk
- loader/disassembly testing through exec and exec -d

Choose CupidC when you want:
- zero-build source execution
- REPL integration
- TempleOS-style source-first workflow
>endtree

